### When does CompletableFuture improve performance?

CF improves performance when you have independent IO-bound tasks that can run in parallel.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.util.concurrent.*;
import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class ParallelBenefit {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        ExecutorService ioPool = Executors.newFixedThreadPool(10);

        // Sequential: 200 + 300 + 150 = 650ms
        // Parallel: max(200, 300, 150) = 300ms

        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> balanceCF = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> fetchBalance(), ioPool);      // 200ms
        CompletableFuture<Integer> scoreCF = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> fetchCreditScore(), ioPool);   // 300ms
        CompletableFuture<BigDecimal> propertyCF = CompletableFuture
            .supplyAsync(() -> fetchPropertyValue(), ioPool); // 150ms

        CompletableFuture.allOf(balanceCF, scoreCF, propertyCF).join();
        // All done in ~300ms instead of 650ms!

        ioPool.shutdown();
    }

    static BigDecimal fetchBalance() {
        sleep(200); return new BigDecimal("500000");
    }
    static int fetchCreditScore() {
        sleep(300); return 750;
    }
    static BigDecimal fetchPropertyValue() {
        sleep(150); return new BigDecimal("3500000");
    }
    static void sleep(long ms) {
        try { Thread.sleep(ms); } catch (InterruptedException e) {
            Thread.currentThread().interrupt();
        }
    }
}
```

### When does CompletableFuture NOT help?

#### Sequential dependencies: no parallelism possible

```java
// Each step needs the previous result. Still sequential!
CompletableFuture<String> result = CompletableFuture
    .supplyAsync(() -> getAccountId("Rajesh"))       // 100ms
    .thenCompose(id -> fetchBalanceAsync(id))          // 200ms, needs id
    .thenApply(balance -> calculateInterest(balance))  // 50ms, needs balance
    .thenApply(interest -> formatReport(interest));     // 10ms, needs interest
// Total: 100 + 200 + 50 + 10 = 360ms
// Direct calls: also 360ms, but simpler and no CF overhead
```

#### Immediate blocking get()

```java
// POINTLESS: blocks immediately
String data = CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> dbQuery()).get();
// Equivalent to (but slower than):
String data = dbQuery();
// The .get() blocks the caller. No parallelism. Extra overhead.
```

#### CPU-bound on small data

```java
// For small lists, parallel overhead exceeds computation
long sum = smallList.parallelStream().mapToLong(this::compute).sum();
// Parallel overhead: thread scheduling, splitting, merging
// If compute() takes < 0.1ms per item and list has < 1000 items,
// sequential is faster!
```

### What is the decision guide?

| Scenario | CF Benefit | Recommendation |
|---|---|---|
| 3 independent HTTP calls | 3x speedup | Use CF with allOf |
| Fan-out to 10 services | 10x speedup | Use CF with allOf |
| Non-blocking pipeline | Caller freed | Use CF pipeline |
| Sequential A then B then C | No speedup | Direct calls |
| supplyAsync().get() | No benefit | Direct call |
| CPU-bound small data | Negative | Sequential loop |
| Trivial 1ms operation | Negative | Direct call |

### How do you identify parallelizable calls?

Draw a dependency graph. Independent nodes (no edges between them) can run in parallel. Dependent chains must be sequential.

```text
Loan Approval:
  fetchCreditScore ──────┐
  fetchPropertyValue ─────┼──▶ decide()
  fetchIncomeProof ───────┘

All three are independent: use CF.allOf()
Latency: max(score, property, income) instead of sum
```

### What are common mistakes?

```java
// WRONG: wrapping every call in CF "just because"
CompletableFuture.supplyAsync(() -> simpleCalculation()).join();
// Overhead > benefit for a 1ms calculation

// WRONG: sequential calls forced into CF pipeline
cf.thenCompose(a -> fetchB(a)).thenCompose(b -> fetchC(b));
// Still sequential! Simpler as: B b = fetchB(a); C c = fetchC(b);

// CORRECT: identify independence, use allOf()
CF<A> a = supplyAsync(() -> fetchA(), pool);
CF<B> b = supplyAsync(() -> fetchB(), pool);
CF.allOf(a, b).join();
// True parallel benefit!
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- Can CF make sequential code faster? Only if the caller can do other work while the pipeline runs (true non-blocking pattern).
- When is the overhead of CF noticeable? For operations under 1ms, the thread switch and object creation overhead is significant.
- What is the minimum latency savings to justify CF? Generally, when parallel calls save at least 50ms compared to sequential.
